#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""yggdrasil-install-system: installs the operating system from its live medium (archiso) onto a disk. Yggdrasil's installer
window runs it as root (pkexec) and shows what it says; it can also be run by hand. Nothing is asked here: the choices come as
one JSON object on standard input, so passwords never appear on a command line.

This Python script is kept for a rollback: the program installed is its Rust port (packages/install-system), which does the
same with the same plan, messages, programs and files; its parity/compare.py runs both on made-up computers.

    {"disk": "/dev/nvme0n1", "mode": "erase",
     "encrypt": true, "encrypt_password": "...",          (LUKS2; one password at start-up)
     "user": {"name": "Full Name", "login": "me", "password": "..."},
     "hostname": "my-pc", "timezone": "Europe/Berlin", "locale": "en_US.UTF-8", "keymap": "us", "keymap_variant": "",
     "browsers": ["vivaldi", "librewolf"], "default_browser": "vivaldi",      (optional; ids from the OS's browser list)
     "extras": ["heimdall"]}                                                  (optional; ids from the OS's optional parts)

    Partitions chosen by hand instead of the whole disk ("mode": "manual"; only on the chosen disk):
    {"disk": "/dev/nvme0n1", "mode": "manual", "new_table": false,
     "delete": [4],                                       (partition numbers, removed first)
     "create": [{"id": "a", "start": 512110190592, "size": 214748364800, "kind": "linux"}],   (bytes; kind linux|efi|swap)
     "use": [{"part": "/dev/nvme0n1p1", "mount": "/boot/efi", "format": null},                  (an existing EFI partition, kept)
             {"part": "new:a", "mount": "/", "format": "btrfs"}],
     "encrypt": true, "encrypt_password": "...", ...}    (encryption is for / only)

    yggdrasil-install-system --check < plan.json      only check the plan, change nothing
    yggdrasil-install-system --disks                  the disks it could install onto (JSON), with their partitions and free space

What it prints, one line each (the installer reads these):
    STEP n N text        step n of N has started
    INFO text            something worth knowing
    WARN text            something went wrong but the install goes on
    ERROR text           the install stopped (nothing after this)
    DONE                 finished: the computer can restart into the new system

The steps: check, partition (a 1 GiB EFI partition and the rest for the system), encrypt (optional, LUKS2), format (btrfs with
the subvolumes @, @home, @cache, @log), copy the system from the medium, set it up inside (the OS's clean-up program, language,
keyboard, time zone, name, the account; root stays locked and the account administers with sudo), the start-up image, GRUB, and
the OS's finishing program. In "manual" mode the partitions are the ones chosen (an existing EFI partition, Windows' for one, is
used as it is: GRUB is added beside what is there and the firmware's fallback copy is left alone).

What belongs to the operating system comes from its installer integration file (the first of kind "installer", as the window
picks it; Yggdrasil's docs/INSTALLER.md) and /etc/os-release, so the same program installs any system built on Yggdrasil:

    kind = installer
    command = /usr/bin/yggdrasil-install-system
    name = Example                       shown in messages, the boot menu and the disk labels (default: NAME in os-release, less " OS")
    medium = /run/archiso/bootmnt/example/x86_64/airootfs.sfs          the system to copy (default: from ID in os-release)
    cleanup = /usr/local/bin/example-install-cleanup                   run inside the new system first: takes away the live medium's
                                                                       account, sign-in and no-password rules
    finish = /usr/local/bin/example-install-finish                     run inside the new system last (a failure stops the install)
    boot_menu_theme = /usr/local/bin/example-grub-theme                run inside after the plain boot menu is written
    kernel_options = quiet splash                                      (default: quiet)
    groups = users wheel video audio kvm                               the account's groups (those the system has)
    reserved_logins = heimdall sarina                                  account names not offered (every existing account is taken too)
    min_size_gb = 30                                                   the smallest disk (default 30)
    browsers = /usr/share/OS/installer-browsers.json                   the Browsers page (also read by the window)
    extras = /usr/share/OS/installer-extras.json                       the optional parts page (also read by the window)
    components = /usr/share/OS/components.json                         what each optional part installs
    first_login = /var/lib/OS/first-login-components.json              where chosen parts the medium lacks are written down for the
                                                                       new user's first login (without it they are left for Agora)
"""
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time

